🚀 𝗗𝗮𝘆 𝟮𝟯/𝟯𝟬 – 𝗔𝗻𝘀𝗶𝗯𝗹𝗲 𝗩𝗮𝗿𝗶𝗮𝗯𝗹𝗲𝘀 & 𝗩𝗮𝗿𝗶𝗮𝗯𝗹𝗲 𝗣𝗿𝗲𝗰𝗲𝗱𝗲𝗻𝗰𝗲

Continuing my hashtag#30DaysOfAnsible journey! 🔥

Today I focused on another fundamental Ansible concept — Variables.

🎯 𝗪𝗵𝗮𝘁 𝗮𝗿𝗲 𝗔𝗻𝘀𝗶𝗯𝗹𝗲 𝗩𝗮𝗿𝗶𝗮𝗯𝗹𝗲𝘀?

Variables allow us to store values and reuse them across our playbooks.

For example:

vars:
 app_name: nginx
 app_port: 8080

tasks:
 - name: Install application
 ansible.builtin.apt:
 name: "{{ app_name }}"
 state: present

Instead of hardcoding values everywhere, we can define them once and reuse them.

🔄 𝗪𝗵𝗲𝗿𝗲 𝗖𝗮𝗻 𝗪𝗲 𝗗𝗲𝗳𝗶𝗻𝗲 𝗩𝗮𝗿𝗶𝗮𝗯𝗹𝗲𝘀?

Variables can come from different sources, including:

📌 Playbook variables
📌 Inventory variables
📌 Group variables
📌 Host variables
📌 Role variables
📌 Extra variables

This is where Variable Precedence becomes important.

🏆 𝗩𝗮𝗿𝗶𝗮𝗯𝗹𝗲 𝗣𝗿𝗲𝗰𝗲𝗱𝗲𝗻𝗰𝗲

When the same variable is defined in multiple places, Ansible follows a precedence hierarchy to determine which value wins.

One important example is:

ansible-playbook site.yml -e "app_port=9090"

-e / --extra-vars has very high precedence and can override lower-precedence variable definitions.

💡 𝗪𝗵𝘆 𝗨𝘀𝗲 𝗩𝗮𝗿𝗶𝗮𝗯𝗹𝗲𝘀?

✔️ Avoid hardcoding
✔️ Make playbooks reusable
✔️ Support different environments
✔️ Simplify configuration management
✔️ Make automation easier to maintain

🌍 𝗥𝗲𝗮𝗹-𝗪𝗼𝗿𝗹𝗱 𝗘𝘅𝗮𝗺𝗽𝗹𝗲

The same playbook can be used for:

🧪 Development → dev values
🧪 Testing → test values
🚀 Production → prod values

Only the variables change — the automation logic can remain the same.

🎯 𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗟𝗲𝗮𝗿𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗴

Good Ansible automation separates configuration from logic.

Once you understand variables and precedence, your playbooks become much more flexible, reusable, and production-friendly.
